WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.810
- [Mark] All right, Wren.
- Yo.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.476
- [Mark] Wren, where'd you
grow up, where are you from?

00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:07.140
- [Wren] I'm from Middle Fork, Kentucky.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.760
- [Mark] And tell me about your childhood,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.980
you had both your parents
when you were a kid?

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.010
- When I was childhood,

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.730
I was son of Dave Bree.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.860
And my mother was Geraldine Bree.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:19.990
- [Mark] Who we just spoke to.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.170
- Yeah, from Hilton,
Kentucky here in Middle Fork.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.003
- [Mark] Right.

00:00:24.003 --> 00:00:25.494
And tell me about your childhood.

00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:27.216
How would you describe your childhood?

00:00:27.216 --> 00:00:28.480
- When I was back in my childhood,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.327
I rode up as a little boy,
I always liked all them,

00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:34.350
me and my little gold cars
had all the wheels and stuff

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.410
and I get on these little county roads,

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.430
just dirt roads back then.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.030
We rode up and down on them,

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.033
and had loads of German
shepherd puppy dogs.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:45.940
That made the matters real interesting.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.190
'Cause they stayed right with me.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.623
Every which way I'd go, they'd go with me.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.900
- [Mark] You of Appalachia?

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.237
You grew up here and just--

00:00:54.237 --> 00:00:55.323
- Yeah, I grewed up in the Appalachian.

00:00:55.323 --> 00:00:57.580
- [Mark] And you chose
to stay here, not leave.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.080
- Yeah, I choose to stay here.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.680
I always wanted work life,
so that's what I've done.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:03.880
I made a worker out of myself.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.230
- [Mark] What kind of stuff
have you done for work?

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.470
- I worked in coal mines,

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.370
I worked in coal mines for about 17 years.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.990
I've done a lot of work in the coal mines

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:12.823
and done a lot of work

00:01:12.823 --> 00:01:15.563
around the coal mines,
inside and outside both.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.330
I've done all kinds of work life.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.957
In my childhood life, I had rode a...

00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:22.150
You know, I was garden,

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.900
my daddy showed me how to work the gardens

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.940
so I worked gardens a lot.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.100
So that made me a farmer,
kind of like a farmer.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.530
And not much farming goin' on here,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.450
but kind of farming we'd done,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.742
you try to make a little money on it.

00:01:34.742 --> 00:01:35.760
Back in them days.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.470
So we growin' up to make money.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.093
That's what it was all
about when I was 14, 15.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.877
- [Mark] I mean, poverty is almost like,

00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:47.900
every household has to deal with.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.730
- Yeah, it was bad back in them days.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.350
I mean, nobody had money around here.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.230
So the only way you could get money

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.369
was get out and work in these hills.

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:56.210
Make this...

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.852
There was illegal stuff here

00:01:57.852 --> 00:01:58.930
and you could make money off of it.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.030
- [Mark] The illegal stuff.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.840
- Yeah, the illegal stuff.
- Like what, like pot?

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.370
- Yeah, marijuana, pot.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.717
Yeah, we growed it and
made a little money,

00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:07.460
you know, that kept us going year to year.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.300
There wasn't no way to make it.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.170
There was nothing here in these mountains.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.070
These Appalachian Mountains,

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:14.620
there was nothing here but just,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.070
and that's how we made it.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.980
We growed a little pot
and sold it outta here

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.850
and it made us a little
money and kept us going.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.800
Should've legalized it a long time ago.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.633
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:26.140
But it's still illegal today.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.720
- Yeah, still illegal today, yeah.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.930
But still you have people
foolin' around a little bit,

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.263
not much as they used to have did,

00:02:31.263 --> 00:02:34.400
but just a little make a
little money along, so.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.233
Somethin' in life.

00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:36.670
- [Mark] And drugs are a big deal here.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:37.503
- Yeah, drugs are a big deal here, yes.

00:02:37.503 --> 00:02:39.560
- [Mark] The crystal
meth has come in and...

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.287
- Yeah, it's no good, that stuff is.

00:02:41.287 --> 00:02:42.500
- [Mark] And the pills are,

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.540
the painkillers have been around

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.920
for a couple decades, right?

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.790
- Yes, that stuff's
been around a long time.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.970
It's not real good for people,

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.200
and people are just kids.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.140
Stuff like that, we don't
allow that 'round here.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.160
We don't want--
- You think the reason,

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:54.993
the reason so many people

00:02:54.993 --> 00:02:56.300
go to drugs is because of how--

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.098
- 'Cause there's nothing to do here.

00:02:58.098 --> 00:02:58.931
- Nothing to do here.
- They know

00:02:58.931 --> 00:03:01.120
there's nothing here but drugs.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.770
Pretty percentage is 95% drugs here

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.130
and a lot of peoples own those drugs

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:06.963
'cause there's nothing else to do.

00:03:06.963 --> 00:03:10.690
You know, you have no gymnasiums,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.860
no more for people to play ball
or play any kind of sports.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.143
It's all what they had.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.010
Pretty much, yeah, drugs just took over.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:21.843
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.490
Have you had any prison time or jail time

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:26.323
because of drugs?

00:03:26.323 --> 00:03:29.110
- No, there wasn't prison
for drugs in my life.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:32.650
Never was and never
been in jail for drugs.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:33.901
- [Mark] Do you have kids of your own?

00:03:33.901 --> 00:03:34.953
- Yeah, I got two fine boys.

00:03:34.953 --> 00:03:37.690
Jonathan Blake Bree and Josh Bree.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.730
- [Mark] How are they doing?
- Oh, they're doing great.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.360
Got one of them a school teacher,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.290
teaches at school.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.513
And got the other one,
works for the state of,

00:03:46.513 --> 00:03:48.800
the state, works for the state.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.112
- [Mark] That's great, good for you.

00:03:51.112 --> 00:03:52.940
- Works for--

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.090
- [Mark] How much money
do you live on a year now?

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:57.620
- Yearly, I live on about $1000 a month.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.210
I live on about $12, $14,000 a year.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.043
- [Mark] Really?

00:04:01.043 --> 00:04:02.773
- I just drive, I drive over

00:04:02.773 --> 00:04:04.800
just what the commonwealth gives me.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.410
It's just, it's my own road.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.272
- [Mark] What was your favorite thing

00:04:08.272 --> 00:04:09.105
about living in Appalachia?

00:04:09.105 --> 00:04:11.430
- Favorite thing living in Appalachians,

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.431
real quiet back in here
and I can breed my dogs.

00:04:14.431 --> 00:04:17.630
And just turn my dogs
loose and let them go wild.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.020
These mountains, these creeks,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.331
and it's about the happiest place

00:04:20.331 --> 00:04:21.423
I've ever been in my life.

00:04:21.423 --> 00:04:22.479
- [Mark] It's beautiful here.

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:24.120
- Yeah, and it's beautiful, it's quiet,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.050
and everybody's friendly here.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.520
What, people ain't on drugs,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.040
they're real friendly
and real good to you.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.490
They're real good people,

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.403
in Leslie and Clay and Harlan.

00:04:35.394 --> 00:04:36.253
- [Mark] Do you ever wonder

00:04:36.253 --> 00:04:37.086
what your life would have been like

00:04:37.086 --> 00:04:39.170
if you had been born in a
big city, like Atlanta or--

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.120
- One time I left here.
- Charlotte.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.590
- One time I left here in about 1990.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:47.307
I was about 18 years old, 17,

00:04:47.307 --> 00:04:50.570
and I took off and went to Dallas, Texas

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:51.570
down in the big city.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.200
One time in my life, never
been there before in my life.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.930
Went down there one
time, and boy, I had fun,

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:57.810
stayed three months down there.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.270
On the first day down, I met a black guy.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.630
And I never thought I'd ever get to talk

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:03.623
to black people like this.

00:05:03.623 --> 00:05:07.210
Gave me a job, and took good
care of me down in Dallas.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.950
And I stayed there
three and a half months,

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.660
and tracked and preyed back to the hills.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.560
Couldn't stay, had to come back.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.351
Had to come back to the Appalachians.

00:05:15.351 --> 00:05:16.184
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:05:16.184 --> 00:05:18.012
- So now I got, now I breed those dogs

00:05:18.012 --> 00:05:21.530
and I breed labs and the name of my dogs,

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.870
Appalachian Labrador Retrievers.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:25.793
And I love raising dogs.

00:05:26.943 --> 00:05:28.427
- [Mark] So do you consider
yourself a hillbilly?

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:29.531
- Yeah, I'm a hillbilly.

00:05:29.531 --> 00:05:30.410
(Mark laughs)
- Yeah, I'm a

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:31.243
Kentucky hillbilly.

00:05:31.243 --> 00:05:32.076
- [Mark] And you're proud of it.

00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:32.950
- Proud of it, yeah.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.950
- [Mark] How do you define a
hillbilly? What is a hillbilly?

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.070
- A hillbilly's when people ask you out,

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.490
swing on grapevines in these mountains

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.380
and climb trees like Tarzan and have fun.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.420
And the mountains is mountains,

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.680
you don't have to take nothing
in the mountains to have fun.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.690
They're history, they're free,

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.357
dig roots and all kinds of stuff.

00:05:50.357 --> 00:05:53.440
We dig roots, cohosh, black cohosh

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.910
like the Indians used
to back in the old days

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.670
to cure people's sickness.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:01.600
We dig ginseng, we're big
and bad at it when we go.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.160
(Mark laughs)

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:04.398
- [Crew Member] Welcome today.

00:06:04.398 --> 00:06:06.674
- I've got the bag on.

00:06:06.674 --> 00:06:07.860
- [Mark] So this is ginseng that you--

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.120
- Okay, yeah, this is way
back on Appalachian Mountains,

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.000
on the ginseng.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.320
Me and my dogs and my brothers and us,

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.690
we all go in these hills,

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:20.430
and we dig this ginseng
to send over to China,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:21.263
cross the water,

00:06:21.263 --> 00:06:23.690
so the China people can
have good things to eat.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:25.620
Like, these big four plugs,

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.430
like stuff like this right here,

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.890
they love it over there.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.290
And it's not like anywhere else you go,

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:33.350
you will find big roots

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:37.610
out of the mountains of
Leslie County, like this.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.470
The ginseng quality's real high,

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.549
it's like 2000 a pound over in China.

00:06:41.549 --> 00:06:42.382
- [Mark] And what do you guys sell it for?

00:06:42.382 --> 00:06:44.390
- And over here, they rob
us and take it from us.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:47.520
We get only about 400 a pound for it

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.930
over here in Kentucky.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:50.780
But you know, we should
get more than that,

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.120
but we're poor and you know,

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.060
we just, we let 'em have it.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.010
What makes us happy?

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.540
We got it right here
forever and ever and ever.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.720
It's right here for us,

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.894
and we get to get it and
then send it all to them.

00:07:01.894 --> 00:07:03.360
(Mark chuckles)

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.883
- That's what we love to
do back in these hills.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:08.223
We love it.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.280
Yeah, here in the
mountains of Leslie County,

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.810
the ginseng, it's real strong.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.960
The juice in it is strong.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:20.020
The Appalachians is the,
it's like marijuana,

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:21.550
the marijuana is better here

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.200
in the Appalachian
Mountains like ginseng is.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.017
That's why everybody wants it for,

00:07:26.017 --> 00:07:27.650
'cause it's better.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.990
We always grow the best,
we always have the best.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.290
I guess if you got the best,

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:35.084
you know, all you want to
do is have all the rest.

00:07:35.084 --> 00:07:37.148
- [Mark] Is it pronounced
Appalachia or Appalachia?

00:07:37.148 --> 00:07:38.164
- Appalachia.

00:07:38.164 --> 00:07:38.999
Appalachia.

00:07:38.999 --> 00:07:39.832
- [Mark] Appalachia.

00:07:39.832 --> 00:07:40.665
- Yeah.

00:07:40.665 --> 00:07:41.862
- [Mark] Okay, I hear different
from different people, but--

00:07:41.862 --> 00:07:42.695
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:44.222
Well, some people call it Appalachian.

00:07:44.222 --> 00:07:45.055
- Yeah, right.
- You know,

00:07:45.055 --> 00:07:45.888
some people, Appalachian.

00:07:45.888 --> 00:07:47.210
See, mine, I got the dogs.

00:07:48.253 --> 00:07:50.520
You know, Appalachian Labrador Retrievers.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.580
And I got all the dogs

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:52.993
that I raised today for 'em.

00:07:52.993 --> 00:07:57.580
I got about nine female
dogs and four males.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.200
And I breed them just,

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.360
in these mountains, you got
to have something to do.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.040
So I breed these dogs and take them

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.310
ginseng-ing with me and
stuff in the mountains.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.240
It's real fun to do that.
- All right.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.020
Appalachia has kind of been left behind.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.050
The rest of the world,
the technology has moved--

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.580
- It's moved forward.
- Advanced very forward.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.340
But here, you're kind of still.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.130
There's no cell service
here, and there's--

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.971
- No cell service in these mountains.

00:08:19.971 --> 00:08:22.843
Just very certain places
you get cell service,

00:08:22.843 --> 00:08:24.944
it's not like the cities.
- [Mark] You have very basic,

00:08:24.944 --> 00:08:25.777
like grocery stores are,

00:08:25.777 --> 00:08:28.860
they're really just gas
stations with like--

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.220
- Grocery stores here,

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:34.520
you have to spend $20, $25
out here to get to one.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.260
To get your food, so if you
just want a pop or a cake

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.420
or something like that,

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:40.700
it's gon' cost you $20 to just get out

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:42.770
to get that cake here in
these Appalachian Mountains.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:44.952
It's a long ways in and out of here.

00:08:44.952 --> 00:08:47.350
Time you get back up here to us to see us,

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:48.980
you're out of a lot of money.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:50.090
- [Mark] Do you feel out of place

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:51.151
when you go to a big city?

00:08:51.151 --> 00:08:52.410
- [Wren] Yeah, I feel out of place, yeah.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.743
It's a lot different.

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:55.923
You're more free back up in here,

00:08:55.923 --> 00:08:58.470
like it's the free world and free land.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.230
You're more free up here in the mountains

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.290
than you are out in the cities.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.570
You got all those people in
the city around you up there.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:05.610
Back in here, you don't have that.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.437
All you got back in here is like

00:09:07.437 --> 00:09:09.980
all the mountains and the mountain bears.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.343
We have the black bears here.
- [Mark] Yeah, you have bears,

00:09:12.343 --> 00:09:13.340
you have elk, you have deer,

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:14.800
you have coyotes, you have--

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.950
- [Wren] Yeah, we have all the cows

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.600
and we have a owl, got
squirrels, and ducks.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.470
We got all kinds of animals
here in the mountains.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.210
- [Mark] Yeah, your mom
was telling me you guys--

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:25.860
- [Wren] We got flying squirrels.

00:09:27.598 --> 00:09:31.210
We got coons, we got the skunks.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.817
- [Mark] And you eat a
lot of these animals.

00:09:32.817 --> 00:09:34.460
- [Wren] We eat a lot
of these animals, yeah.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.110
The coon, you can eat the coon,

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:37.410
it's real good.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.805
You can eat the groundhogs,

00:09:39.805 --> 00:09:41.725
a lot of people eat the groundhogs.

00:09:41.725 --> 00:09:44.757
The ducks, you eat all of 'em.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:48.190
There's all kinds of animals

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:49.890
in the mountains that you can eat.

00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:52.950
A lot of people also eat the black bear,

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.820
they kill it, they eat it.
- [Mark] You can eat bear?

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:57.100
- Yeah, they eat bear here too, yeah.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.110
Elk, they eat the elk.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.180
- [Mark] Yeah, interesting.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.711
So a lot of characters out here,

00:10:02.711 --> 00:10:04.583
a lot of people get in
trouble with the law,

00:10:04.583 --> 00:10:05.416
and a lot of people--

00:10:05.416 --> 00:10:06.357
- Yeah, a lot of law right here

00:10:06.357 --> 00:10:09.310
that's been going on for
last five to 10 years,

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:10.143
goin' on 'round here.

00:10:10.143 --> 00:10:12.270
- [Mark] A lot of like
outlaw personalities, right?

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.890
- Yeah, outlaw personalities,
you got a lot--

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.580
- [Mark] The Confederate flag
to a lot of people means...

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:19.510
It's symbolic of racism.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.130
But out here, the Confederate flag

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.190
means something very different, right?

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:23.023
- Very different, yeah.

00:10:23.023 --> 00:10:24.880
- [Mark] It means like, you're an outlaw.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.290
- Yeah, we were the rebel flag is,

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:27.220
will be a rebel outlaw.
- It has nothing

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.410
to do with racism.

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:29.998
- No, it has nothing to do with racism.

00:10:29.998 --> 00:10:30.831
No, we don't--
- Right.

00:10:30.831 --> 00:10:31.664
There's a lot of people--

00:10:31.664 --> 00:10:34.460
- I'm not prejudiced
with the black people.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:37.720
When I wore the rebel flag
for the last 20 years,

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:39.033
I never thought about one time

00:10:39.033 --> 00:10:41.250
that it even concerned a black person.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:42.083
- [Mark] Right.

00:10:42.083 --> 00:10:42.926
- And back in Middle where we come from.

00:10:42.926 --> 00:10:43.759
- [Mark] I think that's
a big misunderstanding,

00:10:43.759 --> 00:10:45.130
people see the Confederate flag

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.760
and they think, "Oh, that
means you're a racist."

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:47.770
- Yeah, that's wrong.

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:48.667
- It doesn't mean--
- No, it doesn't.

00:10:48.667 --> 00:10:50.820
And the black people should
understand that immediately,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:51.653
that's wrong.

00:10:51.653 --> 00:10:52.486
- [Mark] Absolutely.

00:10:52.486 --> 00:10:53.650
- We wear blue.

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.540
Blue is for black,

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.690
that's why I got black, blue on today is,

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:59.527
a lot of people won't
wear blue like I do today

00:10:59.527 --> 00:11:01.790
because it's for the black boys,

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.020
but I'm not prejudiced.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:04.551
I wear all colors.

00:11:04.551 --> 00:11:05.598
- [Mark] And you'd wear a
Confederate flag on your shirt?

00:11:05.598 --> 00:11:07.120
- I wear red, white, and blue.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:07.990
- [Mark] You'd wear a Confederate flag

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:08.942
right now and be proud of it?

00:11:08.942 --> 00:11:11.429
- Be proud of it, yes, I
will. I got one to wear.

00:11:11.429 --> 00:11:12.262
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:11:12.262 --> 00:11:13.190
- Yeah, I got one.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.640
I always wear it ever since
I've been 16 year old,

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:16.960
I've had a flag on my head

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:18.810
and wore it every day they ever since,

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:19.940
and I'll wear it from now on.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:23.050
And they have no, no whatsoever racism

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:25.253
against no black people, never.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:28.910
Everybody say I'm nationwide.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.826
Everybody likes me 'cause I'm nationwide.

00:11:30.826 --> 00:11:32.160
Everybody calls me nationwide.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.540
I know everybody.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:34.610
I know how all kin,

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:36.409
I am to all kinds of people 'round here.

00:11:36.409 --> 00:11:37.390
- [Mark] You know everybody in the county?

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:38.223
- Yeah, just about.

00:11:38.223 --> 00:11:39.530
Everybody in the county.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:40.543
Oh, I know, I'm nationwide,

00:11:40.543 --> 00:11:43.330
I know everybody all over
the states in other states.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.059
I know a lot of people,

00:11:45.059 --> 00:11:48.930
I get to know a lot of people.
I'm antisocial, but I ain't.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.000
I am social everybody.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.460
They say, "Well, you antisocial."

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:54.670
No, you're not, you're
sociable with everybody.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.350
So that makes me well, nationwide.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.290
I like to know everybody
and good to everybody.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:01.613
I want respect like I give respect.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.230
If you give respect, you'll get respect.

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:08.407
If you're good to people,
good'll come back to you.

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:14.640
My granny told me seven times
more great will come to you

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:15.990
when you're good to people.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:17.783
Yeah.

00:12:17.783 --> 00:12:19.675
- [Mark] Wren, thank you so
much for talking with me.

00:12:19.675 --> 00:12:20.508
- Yeah.

00:12:20.508 --> 00:12:21.500
- [Mark] You're fascinating.

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:23.070
- [Wren] Yeah, I could talk some more.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:24.610
I know a lot, I'm nationwide.

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.510
I know a lot I could sit and tell you.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.837
I know some real good stories
I could tell that's true.

00:12:29.837 --> 00:12:30.670
- [Mark] All right, we'll
come back next time.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:32.730
- [Wren] Yeah, come back
and get me on the next one

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:34.340
and put me on YouTube again,

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:35.553
they love me there.

00:12:35.553 --> 00:12:36.760
- [Mark] All right, Wren.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:37.593
Thank you very much.

00:12:37.593 --> 00:12:39.210
- [Wren] Hey, thank you so much, sir.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.324
My sister talked about it for six months.

00:12:42.324 --> 00:12:43.282
- [Mark] Next time.

00:12:43.282 --> 00:12:44.365
- [Wren] Bye.

